00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.690
So far in the series, we
learned about Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.870
how it works using Cat-Out's
asynchronous order processing

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:22.710
service.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.370
We saw how pub/sub helps manage
the interactions between order,

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.370
packaging, shipping, and
notification services.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.189
In the last episode, we learned
that pub/sub supports both

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.680
push and pull message delivery.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.050
Let's do a quick recap.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.950
Push delivery
requires the client

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.010
to be a public HTTPS endpoint.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.630
If you don't have
one, which means

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.490
your service is private
or firewalls are involved,

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:46.940
then you would use pull.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.860
Pull is also more efficient
and minimizes latency.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.740
So if events are
generated at, say,

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.440
100 or 1,000
requests per second,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.010
then pull is your friend.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.640
So today, let's focus
on Cat-Out's services

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.860
to learn pull subscribers.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.230
Let's say it is the
high season for Cat-Out.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.170
Lots of people are ordering
pretty cat posters,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.210
which means the
packaging service needs

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.630
to process this huge
traffic with high efficiency

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.090
and low latency.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.530
And we also know that the
packaging service is internal

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.560
and does not have a
public HTTPS endpoint.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.690
Given these conditions--
large requests

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.000
per second, low
latency requirements,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.020
and no public HTTPS endpoint--
the best way to setup

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.660
our packaging subscriber
is in pull mode.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.530
The pub/sub service offers two
APIs for retrieving messages

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.520
in pull mode.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.300
There's a pull API and there
is a streaming pull API.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.310
The default is
the streaming pull

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.560
API, which is the
most effective way

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.250
to achieve high-volume
processing asynchronously.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.520
And for this reason,
default client libraries

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.670
just use streaming pull for
maximum throughput and lowest

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.080
latency.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.960
Streaming pull is
actually a lot like push,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.220
in that it allows pub/sub to
push messages to the client

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.990
as they become available.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.610
The difference here
is that the client

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.310
gets to initiate this
bidirectional persistent

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.330
connection.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.700
The protocol here is
also a lot more efficient

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.330
than HTTP POST used by push.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.770
Cat-Out's packaging
service requires

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.120
high-volume processing.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.490
And the best way to achieve
high-volume processing

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.690
is by processing asynchronously.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:26.280
So packaging servers should just
use default client libraries

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.800
that use streaming
pull API and offers

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.690
maximum throughput
and low latency.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:35.160
Now that we know how a message
is received by pull subscriber,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.680
let's double-click on
the message itself.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.390
The messages sent
by the publisher

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.220
can contain custom attributes.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.900
And in subscriber
code, you will have

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.820
to process those attributes.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.250
In Cat-Out's case, say we
are sending the message

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.903
from order service to
packaging subscriber.

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:51.570
There would be custom
message attributes

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.350
to include, like user_id,
city, and poster_id.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:56.940
Let's see how to
send and receive

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.700
these message attributes.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.460
We will use the same quick start
code we used in episode three.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.440
If you've not seen
it, now is the time

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.480
to check it out, and
then continue on here.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.970
We are in our Python
quickstart folder.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:12.170
Let's open pub.py.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.730
In the pub method,
we will add the logic

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.980
to update the message
to include custom

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.460
attributes-- the user_id,
city, and poster_id.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.060
Then we open two terminals to
start our packaging subscriber

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.060
in one and send the order
message from the order

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:28.960
publisher in the other.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.680
And we see that the message
with the same message ID

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.170
is received by the
packaging subscriber

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.300
with the custom
attributes that were sent.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.300
Now what if suddenly,
Cat-Out becomes famous

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.230
and their order rate
increases rapidly?

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.030
Since messages are being
published at a higher rate

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.710
than they are being
consumed, they

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.050
would have to be
processed fast enough.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.220
If this situation
persists, then Cat-Out

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.300
should consider increasing the
number of subscriber client

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.980
instances to process the
requests in parallel.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.040
But if this is a transient
spike in messages,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.800
say, due to Black Friday or
more cat birthdays in a month,

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.070
then there could be
a few possibilities.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.740
It's possible that one
client could have a backlog

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.730
of messages, but another client
in the network has capacity,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.400
but this free client cannot
process those messages

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.850
because the first client
maintains a lease on them.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.970
It's also possible that the
client libraries repeatedly

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.070
extend the
acknowledgment deadline

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.930
for backlogged messages.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:28.230
Those messages continue
to consume memory, CPU,

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.300
and bandwidth resources.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.870
And eventually, the subscriber
runs out of those resources.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.760
To mitigate these
issues, you need

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.270
to use the flow control
feature of the subscriber.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.210
In this Python example, we
are limiting the subscriber

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:46.290
to only have 10 outstanding
messages at a time.

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.330
In the high-volume
context, it is

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.910
important to mention
that the subscriber also

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.870
support concurrency depending
on the programming language.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.450
For language implementations
that support parallel threads,

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:00.120
such as Java and Go,
the client libraries

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.940
make a default choice for
the number of threads,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.460
but this choice may not be
optimal for your application.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.630
If you find that your subscriber
application is not keeping up

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:13.410
with the incoming message
volume, but is not CPU bound,

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.640
you should increase
the thread count.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:19.030
And for CPU-intensive message
processing operations,

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:22.640
reducing the number of
threads might be appropriate.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.690
Now, there could be times when
you see duplicate messages

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.740
from pub/sub.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:29.140
This usually happens when
you acknowledge a message

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.330
before it's acknowledgment
deadline has expired.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:35.620
So pub/sub resends the
message, leading to duplicates.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.840
You can detect this
behavior in Stackdriver

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.350
by monitoring the acknowledge
operations with the expired

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:42.880
response code.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:46.790
To reduce the duplication rate,
extend the message deadline.

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:49.310
Some client libraries
handle this automatically,

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.940
but if you are building
your own client library,

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.340
then use
modifyAckDeadline method

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.890
to extend it the
acknowledgment deadline.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.840
On the other hand,
if you want to force

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:02.980
pub/sub to retry a message, just
set modifyAckDeadline to zero.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.960
Now, there are cases
where you might

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.360
need to do a bit more
work than just using

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.970
the asynchronous client library
we've been talking about

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:10.930
so far.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:14.500
In those cases, you would
use the synchronous pull API.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.320
It's recommended to use
synchronous pull when

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.560
the application logic might
rely on a polling pattern

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.140
to retrieve messages
or your application

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.360
requires a precise
cap on the number

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.590
of messages retrieved by it.

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.950
Streaming pull at the
moment can overdeliver

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:31.930
before it settles
down, so when you

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.590
have a spiky load of
very small messages,

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:37.180
it can get you in trouble.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.510
Those applications are better
off using synchronous pull.

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:43.600
Or in the third case, if you're
using a language framework,

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.210
like PHP for example,
that does not

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:50.140
support gRPC and
require HTTP REST API,

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:54.280
then you have no choice but
to use synchronous pull.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.280
If we focus our attention
on our Cat-Out application,

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:59.320
due to some resource
constraints,

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:02.620
the notification service is
designed so it can receive

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:06.670
a batch of messages in a single
HTTP request and the number

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.550
of messages cannot
exceed, say, 10.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.860
Based on what we
just learned, Cat-Out

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:15.840
should use synchronous pull
API in this specific scenario.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.740
All right, so today,
we learned how

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:20.440
to receive messages
using pull method.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:22.090
We looked at message
flow control,

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:24.790
concurrency, streaming
pull API, and dealing

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:26.560
with duplicate messages.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.380
Join us next time as we
take on the next topic,

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:31.960
receiving messages using push.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:34.030
Until then, give us
a like, subscribe,

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:37.570
and let us know what you want
to learn about Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:40.620
